// Practice quiz: Fixing N+1 with JOIN FETCH
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/relationships-and-transactions/fixing-n-1-with-join-fetch

::o1 fetch keyword::What does fetch add to a JPQL join? {
=It initializes the association in the same query#Correct. Without fetch, the join only filters.
~It makes the query update rows#Not quite. It does not write.
~It removes the join#Not quite. It keeps the join.
~It disables lazy mapping everywhere#Not quite. It affects only this query.
}

::o1 entity graph::What does @EntityGraph(attributePaths \= "sessions") do? {
~Deletes sessions#Not quite. It only affects loading.
~Changes the mapping to EAGER everywhere#Not quite. It applies to this method only.
~Validates a request#Not quite. That is Bean Validation.
=Loads sessions with the query for this method#Correct. It is a per-use-case fetch plan.
}

::o2 proving::How do you prove an N+1 fix worked? {
~Check that the code compiles#Not quite. Compiling proves nothing here.
~Check the log has no INFO lines#Not quite. That is unrelated.
=Count statements before and after, and check that the count no longer grows with rows#Correct. The evidence is a number.
~Restart and hope#Not quite. That is not evidence.
}

::o3 pagination limit::With Hibernate 7.4, when does paging a query with a collection fetch join fall back to paging in memory? {
~On every database, because a fetch join always multiplies rows#Not quite. Hibernate 7.4 pages in the database where limits in subqueries are supported.
~Never, because a fetch join cannot multiply rows#Not quite. A collection join repeats the parent once per child.
=On a database that does not support limits and offsets in subqueries#Correct. Elsewhere Hibernate pages the parents in a subquery; on such a database it warns and pages in memory, or fails if configured to.
~Only on H2#Not quite. The fallback depends on whether the database supports limits in subqueries, not on one product.
}

::o3 alternative::What is a fitting alternative for a paged list of parents with children? {
~EAGER on the mapping#Not quite. It worsens every other query.
=Batch fetching, or a second query for the children#Correct. The database still pages the parents.
~Removing the page size#Not quite. That loads everything.
~A bigger heap#Not quite. That hides memory use.
}

